    {
      "publicId": "chal_quiz_sqlf_211",
      "name": "join-fan-out-quiz",
      "type": "quiz",
      "title": "Count the rows before you trust the sum",
      "description": "Two small joins from the revenue incident: predict how many rows a join of orders, payments and order items produces and what SUM(total_cents) returns, then pick the queries that give finance's revenue number.",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "points": 10,
      "questions": [
        {
          "type": "single_choice",
          "points": 5,
          "question": "Three orders: order 1 (total_cents 3000), order 2 (2000) and order 3 (1500).\npayments: order 1 has 2 rows, order 2 has 1 row, order 3 has none yet.\norder_items: order 1 has 3 rows, order 2 has 2 rows, order 3 has 1 row.\n\nSELECT COUNT(*), SUM(o.total_cents)\nFROM orders o\nJOIN payments    p ON p.order_id = o.order_id\nJOIN order_items i ON i.order_id = o.order_id;\n\nWhat does it return?",
          "explanation": "A join produces one row for every combination of rows that match. For order 1 that is 2 payments x 3 items = 6 rows, for order 2 it is 1 x 2 = 2 rows, and order 3 has no payment, so the inner join drops it: 8 rows. SUM(o.total_cents) then adds each order total once per row: 6 x 3000 + 2 x 2000 = 22000, while the two orders that have a payment are only worth 5000. Two one-to-many joins from the same table multiply, they do not add.",
          "options": [
            {
              "text": "8 rows, 22000",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "3 rows, 6500",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "8 rows, 5000",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "9 rows, 6500",
              "correct": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "points": 5,
          "question": "orders (all status 'paid'): order 201 total 4500, order 202 total 4500, order 203 total 7000.\npayments: order 201 one payment of 4500, order 202 two payments (2000 + 2500), order 203 one payment of 7000.\n\nFinance wants the total of all orders that have at least one payment: 16000 cents. Which queries return 16000? (Select all that apply.)",
          "explanation": "A plain JOIN to payments repeats order 202 for each of its two payments, so SUM(o.total_cents) gives 20500; a LEFT JOIN keeps unmatched orders but fans out exactly the same way, so it also gives 20500. SUM(DISTINCT o.total_cents) removes repeated values, not repeated orders: orders 201 and 202 both total 4500, so it returns 4500 + 7000 = 11500. Pre-aggregating payments to one row per order before the join keeps one row per order (16000), and IN only tests whether an order has a payment, so it never repeats an order (16000).",
          "options": [
            {
              "text": "SELECT SUM(o.total_cents) FROM orders o JOIN payments p ON p.order_id = o.order_id",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "SELECT SUM(DISTINCT o.total_cents) FROM orders o JOIN payments p ON p.order_id = o.order_id",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "SELECT SUM(o.total_cents) FROM orders o JOIN (SELECT order_id, SUM(amount_cents) AS paid_cents FROM payments GROUP BY order_id) AS p ON p.order_id = o.order_id",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "SELECT SUM(total_cents) FROM orders WHERE order_id IN (SELECT order_id FROM payments)",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "SELECT SUM(o.total_cents) FROM orders o LEFT JOIN payments p ON p.order_id = o.order_id",
              "correct": false
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
